#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:5
#: 8e4c87b684fe49bb9ac2a9e16fc35164
msgid "Extended Kalman Filter Navigation Overview and Tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:9
#: 3c61494342b346139d3f37d87799c694
msgid "**IMPORTANT:** This article is about EKF1 which has been removed from the codebase. It's kept as a reference, as it has been the basis for EKF2 and EKF3. EKF3 is now the default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:14
#: 6fe19171fa4b40efb934dec330fd4f18
msgid "This article describes the Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) algorithm used to estimate vehicle position, velocity and angular orientation based on rate gyroscopes, accelerometer, compass (magnetometer), GPS, airspeed and barometric pressure measurements. It includes both an overview of the algorithm and information about the available tuning parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:22
#: ce64ae8758dd46a3bb9e0f77eefc204e
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:24
#: f6b70d6e178e4878bb450f423311bf1f
msgid "The availability of faster processors (like the one on Pixhawk) have enabled more advanced mathematical algorithms to be implemented to estimate the orientation, velocity and position of the flight vehicle. An Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) algorithm has been developed that uses rate gyroscopes, accelerometer, compass, GPS, airspeed and barometric pressure measurements to estimate the position, velocity and angular orientation of the flight vehicle. This algorithm is implemented in the **AP_NavEKF2** and **AP_NavEKF3** libraries and is based on initial work documented here: https://github.com/priseborough/InertialNav"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:34
#: 8341406fdb6146149f9366d16937901e
msgid "The advantage of the EKF over the simpler complementary filter algorithms used by DCM and Copter's Inertial Nav, is that by fusing all available measurements it is better able to reject measurements with significant errors so that the vehicle becomes less susceptible to faults that affect a single sensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:40
#: 1c2254a1275140a29e28a5952aa4721a
msgid "Another feature of the EKF algorithm is that it is able to estimate offsets in the vehicle's compass readings and also estimate the earth's magnetic field for both plane and copter as well as rover applications. This makes it less sensitive to compass calibration errors than current DCM and INAV algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:46
#: 9481c30abdc842429a27fca3e762a249
msgid "It also enables measurements from optional sensors such as optical flow and laser range finders to be used to assist navigation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:50
#: 973cca609c5d4811b0864e50eff9f987
msgid "Theory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:52
#: 6f00f6f1a7294ebdb743d806f5b005e2
msgid "The EKF (Extended Kalman Filter) algorithm implemented, estimates a total of 22 states with the underlying equations derived using the following: https://github.com/priseborough/InertialNav/blob/master/derivations/GenerateEquations22states.m"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:57
#: 540b10bb78184d7e8e49ea67c84216c6
msgid "The following is a greatly simplified non-mathematical description of how the filter works:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:60
#: 3fe17af8257940918aafa7008c7c4785
msgid "IMU angular rates are integrated to calculate the angular position"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:61
#: fbdbaa5d54244fe49bcfbe4792bd7c17
msgid "IMU accelerations are converted using the angular position from body X,Y,Z to earth North,East and Down axes and corrected for gravity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:63
#: e806fd195d9b49519e53db7bbf58f7f3
msgid "Accelerations are integrated to calculate the velocity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:64
#: 9c22b42309f54029886bd74a10081943
msgid "Velocity is integrated to calculate the position"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:66
#: ffb592beb0334e6984ef24c22ab9958b
msgid "This process from 1) to 4) is referred to as 'State Prediction'. A 'state' is a variable we are trying to estimate like roll, pitch, yaw, height, wind speed, etc. The filter has other states besides position, velocity and angles that are assumed to change slowly. These include gyro biases, Z accelerometer bias, wind velocities, compass biases and the earth's magnetic field. These other states aren't modified directly by the 'State Prediction' step but can be modified by measurements as described later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:74
#: b170631ce48f473f82b71871df2a8510
msgid "Estimated gyro and accelerometer noise (``EKF_GYRO_NOISE`` and ``EKF_ACC_NOISE``) are used to estimate the growth in error in the angles, velocities and position calculated using IMU data. Making these parameters larger causes the filters error estimate to grow faster. If no corrections are made using other measurements (eg GPS), this error estimate will continue to grow. These estimated errors are captured in a large matrix called the 'State Covariance Matrix'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:82
#: 48883d36206f4e9e85f28a6b97a638a9
msgid "Steps 1) to 5) are repeated every time we get new IMU data until a new measurement from another sensor is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:85
#: 3ae5ce3d680f4c27a1e6c24425e9fd11
msgid "If we had a perfect initial estimate, perfect IMU measurements and perfect calculations, then we could keep repeating 1) to 4) throughout the flight with no other calculations required. However, errors in the initial values, errors in the IMU measurements and rounding errors in our calculations mean that we can only go for a few seconds before the velocity and position errors become too large."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:92
#: 90ceee0e84fc4ec8b3f10afd598e23c3
msgid "The Extended Kalman Filter algorithm provides us with a way of combining or fusing data from the IMU, GPS, compass, airspeed, barometer and other sensors to calculate a more accurate and reliable estimate of our position, velocity and angular orientation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:97
#: e546692f3b974168a97757bd1f86b3d1
msgid "The following example describes how GPS horizontal position measurements are used, however the same principal applies to other measurement types (barometric altitude, GPS velocity, etc)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:101
#: 603bcb1fdf1b4a019ec87078eb8da674
msgid "When a GPS measurement arrives, the filter calculates the difference between the predicted position from 4) and the position from the GPS. This difference is called an 'Innovation'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:104
#: d9f6dce57d8749fa82e1d69d0ca65b6f
msgid "The 'Innovation' from 6), 'State Covariance Matrix' from 5), and the GPS measurement error specified by ``EKF_POSNE_NOISE`` are combined to calculate a correction to each of the filter states. This is referred to as a 'State Correction'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:109
#: 15c76f40fd2745c0a3e6f136a39dc6db
msgid "This is the clever part of the a Kalman Filter, as it is able to use knowledge of the correlation between different errors and different states to correct states other than the one being measured. For example GPS position measurements are able to correct errors in position, velocity, angles and gyro bias."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:115
#: 092480fc2fd340beb7cffd99e5f888ee
msgid "The amount of correction is controlled by the assumed ratio of the error in the states to the error in the measurements. This means if the filter thinks its own calculated position is more accurate than the GPS measurement, then the correction from the GPS measurement will be smaller. If it thinks its own calculated position is less accurate than the GPS measurement, then the correction from the GPS measurement will be larger. The assumed accuracy of the GPS measurement is controlled by the ``EKF_POSNE_NOISE``, parameter. Making ``EKF_POSNE_NOISE`` larger causes the filter to think the GPS position is less accurate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:126
#: 669cbafb66db4d6e87164b4b50dd2154
msgid "Because we have now taken a measurement, the amount of uncertainty in each of the states that have been updated is reduced. The filter calculates the reduction in uncertainty due to the 'State Correction', updates the 'State Covariance Matrix' and returns to step 1)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:133
#: dd1f29bb949443fe8fa53610a605ab52
msgid "Tuning Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:138
#: 414bca12a40a42598c6c59e79b1926bd
msgid "AHRS_EKF_USE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:140
#: d2bf430267724feeb915481c90a8bb89
msgid "This should be set to 1 to enable use of the filter, or set to 0 to use the legacy algorithms. Be aware that both algorithms are running regardless of this parameter, and all the EKF data will be logged regardless, provided full rate AHRS data logging is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:145
#: 1ef0011269284aea85053ec365f14ed5
msgid "From Copter3.3 onwards the EKF has been enabled by default and this parameter is not available. Plane and Rover users can still elect to use the legacy algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:150
#: 46526a501ccf4a8188b9a585b04b3d36
msgid "EKF_ABIAS_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:152
#: 4002ded6f17d4466b7dc89d4e9b85b18
msgid "This noise controls the growth of the vertical accelerometer bias state error estimate. Increasing it makes accelerometer bias estimation faster and noisier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:157
#: d299cf60df1e4acca1f18f4beca3acd3
msgid "EKF_ACC_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:159
#: edf66faf974648b9b056aa7a0b23e4c1
msgid "This noise controls the growth of estimated error due to accelerometer measurement errors excluding bias. Increasing it makes the filter trust the accelerometer measurements less and other measurements more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:166
#: d9383da39bb244709d329bc26a448b89
msgid "EKF_ALT_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:168
#: 836cbca66958454e9eda2298748cfe2d
msgid "This is the RMS value of noise in the altitude measurement. If you increase this parameter, the filter will think the barometer is more noisy and will place less weighting on its measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:172
#: 987dc26f86a9456286f2e054113e9853
msgid "If this parameter is set too small, then the filter will constantly react to noise in the barometer measurement which will cause the filter height to be noisy. In copters this will cause the copter to jiggle up and down during altitude hold."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:177
#: ddd1092a77274851b676398304f0a041
msgid "If this parameter is set too high, then the height will tend to wander more and will be more susceptible to GPS vertical velocity glitches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:180
#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:400
#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:442
#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:466
#: 55ad058e4d40440185be4b4d36d7ffbe
#: 27f23463760e4ff58a1e95b87aaa7aed
#: 05840158695c42af89314838aeb42a75
#: 0192326ddbe7495f88ea948296a9a1c5
msgid "See the section on interpreting EKF3 log data for more information on using log data to help set this parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:184
#: 1e76956479964bcfa694d1600ac0f356
msgid "EKF_ALT_SOURCE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:186
#: 373b289415374029835fba8e78e6289e
msgid "This parameter controls which measurement source is used to determine height during optical flow navigation. Set to 0 to use the barometer or to 1 to use the range finder. If set to 1,the vehicle will attempt to maintain a constant height relative to the terrain, which is the default behaviour during optical flow navigation. Warning : EK2_ALT_SOURCE = 1 is only suitable for low altitude and low speed operation over flat surfaces, not for up and away flight. To use range finder at lower altitudes and barometer for up and away flight, set EK2_ALT_SOURCE = 0 and use the RNG_USE_HGT parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:197
#: 5bd5ef98303b4708ab744aedf56840f5
msgid "EKF_EAS_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:199
#: 3ebcf51bcfb64ed0bc8fe280a11a89b4
msgid "This parameter scales the threshold used for the airspeed measurement innovation consistency check. Decreasing it makes it more likely that good measurements will be rejected. Increasing it makes it more likely that bad measurements will be accepted. It is scaled in units of standard deviation. For example a setting of 3 means that differences greater than than 3 x the assumed standard deviation will cause the measurement to be rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:208
#: 9cebb0fffbbc46a2972e570979b2764d
msgid "EKF_EAS_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:210
#: fae90960e11d42e4a5ae82ad00751e63
msgid "This is the RMS value of noise in airspeed measurements. Increasing it reduces the weighting on these measurements. See the section on interpreting EKF3 log data for more information on using log data to help set this parameter. See the section on interpreting EKF3 log data for more information on using log data to help set this parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:217
#: c825f398dd6745329475eb5e0869e37d
msgid "EKF_FALLBACK"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:219
#: aa44debdad0644b2908b16bb57233d2a
msgid "This parameter controls whether inconsistency in sensor data can cause a fallback to DCM. If set to 0, then detection of inconsistent sensor cannot cause a fallback. If set to 1, then large inconsistencies in data will result a fallback to DCM if available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:225
#: 5e66d9b210b94c35b8b26812f01b8288
msgid "EKF_FLOW_DELAY"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:227
#: da59e04236f64f3d9da635008bcd4ad6
msgid "This is the number of msec that the optical flow rate measurements lag behind the IMU measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:231
#: ab245590112d4ea998490ea83affe012
msgid "EKF_FLOW_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:233
#: 1e17330dd7f041ea8f4e81d65cdd542f
msgid "This parameter controls the maximum amount of difference in between the measured optical flow rates and the predicted rates before the EKF starts to reject the measurements. Reducing this parameter makes it more likely that valid optical flow rate measurements will be rejected. Increasing this parameter makes it more likely that invalid optical flow rate measurements will be accepted. It is scaled in units of standard deviation. For example a setting of 3 means that differences greater than than 3 x the assumed standard deviation will cause the measurement to be rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:244
#: 3557523e6e894b068dd2d809bb259644
msgid "EKF_FLOW_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:246
#: a26b7056e6664d9788030e62724969a6
msgid "This parameter allows for optical flow rate measurement errors and noise. It represents the expected RMS error in rad/sec. If set too large the position will drift more. If set too small the position and velocity output from the EKF will become noisy and there is a risk that the EKF could start rejecting optical flow measurements during manoeuvres."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:253
#: b0dbe868c3cc49ae95ae092e5222e730
msgid "EKF_GBIAS_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:255
#: a83af5c61bdb4e75b1196e4068c20267
msgid "This noise controls the growth of gyro bias state error estimates. Increasing it makes rate gyro bias estimation faster and noisier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:259
#: acd94e4e12504d56ab9d4566b4a57734
msgid "EKF_GLITCH_ACCEL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:261
#: 7dd4ed0606324235aab125f2c96584df
msgid "This parameter controls the maximum amount of difference in horizontal acceleration (in cm/s^2) between the value predicted by the filter and the value measured by the GPS, before the GPS position measurement is rejected. If this value is set too low, then valid GPS data will be regularly discarded, and the position accuracy will degrade. If this parameter is set too high, then GPS glitches can cause large rapid changes in position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:270
#: 2370877d6b92496a9437ff16ec80eff4
msgid "EKF_GLITCH_RAD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:272
#: 32658cdc845c4e96b7b8d46b0eba1ccf
msgid "This parameter controls the maximum amount of difference in horizontal position (in m) between the value predicted by the filter and the value measured by the GPS, before the long term glitch protection logic is activated and an offset is applied to the GPS measurement to compensate. Position jumps smaller than this parameter will be temporarily ignored, but if they persist will then be accepted and the filter will move to the new position. Position steps larger than this value, will also be ignored initially, but if they persist, the GPS position measurement will be corrected by the amount of the step before being used. This prevents a large step change in position. This correction is decayed back to zero at a constant rate so that the new GPS position will be realised gradually. The value of this correction in the north and east directions can be checked by plotting the EKF4.OFN and EKF4.OFE flashlog data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:288
#: ad271e751a9d48dd97112af7802f28d9
msgid "EKF_GND_GRADIENT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:290
#: 09d834d6012a420181adcdacb09e7b11
msgid "This parameter controls the amount of terrain gradient in % that is assumed when fusing range finder data and influences how rapidly the estimated terrain height responds to changes in measurement. This can be increased when operating over uneven terrain to allow the terrain estimate to change more rapidly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:299
#: 7846d00864844729be4b0799456aeb6c
msgid "EKF_GPS_TYPE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:301
#: 25a3206d8fd24c8cb90c0f32a2abcec5
msgid "This parameter controls use of GPS velocity measurements : 0 = use 3D velocity, 1 = use 2D velocity, 2 = use no velocity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:305
#: 825e8ee746244843b2907e21f1cf17a9
msgid "EKF_GYRO_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:307
#: ccec22b0508f4e26b5adaec5a192154f
msgid "This noise controls the growth of estimated error due to gyro measurement errors excluding bias. Increasing it makes the filter trust the gyro measurements less and other measurements more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:312
#: ae2d5fea3c624e8787aff41c85167f25
msgid "EKF_HGT_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:314
#: 938d1ffefb4b420ba0cdadab0aa56f51
msgid "This parameter scales the threshold used for the height measurement innovation consistency check. Decreasing it makes it more likely that good measurements will be rejected. Increasing it makes it more likely that bad measurements will be accepted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:320
#: 564faefa7f6a43edabc7f7eaa87b56c3
msgid "EKF_MAGB_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:322
#: 8b440dbab29746b79e3cb35ba5139afe
msgid "This noise controls the growth of body magnetic field state error estimates. Increasing it makes compass offset estimation faster and noisier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:327
#: 49c6e4ccbb0e4c14a290d12c67afedb4
msgid "EKF_MAGE_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:329
#: be0cf52f1d904e0288584cdabd13d923
msgid "This noise controls the growth of earth magnetic field state error estimates. Increasing it makes earth magnetic field bias estimation faster and noisier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:334
#: b2f31fbc02f648c9b56538bd4be11ed1
msgid "EKF_MAG_CAL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:336
#: f1bc392369404a37a2c24d2ccf99a188
msgid "The EKF is capable of learning magnetometer offsets in-flight. This parameter controls when the learning is active:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:339
#: 818738eef02545ecb6ed48ff4e61d15d
msgid "``EKF_MAG_CAL = 0``: Learning is enabled when speed and height indicate the vehicle is airborne"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:341
#: a3e9e36b492b4c80a9bc5b6d96e3ea7d
msgid "``EKF_MAG_CAL = 1``: Learning is enabled when the vehicle is manoeuvring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:343
#: a2b7ff6c3fb143f896bbb1c760f2dc06
msgid "``EKF_MAG_CAL = 2``: Learning is disabled"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:344
#: c6baa7fdb8134fd8aa7e2a1e75c3aa71
msgid "``EKF_MAG_CAL = 3``: Learning is enabled when the vehicle is armed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:347
#: 4cc670bd5ac641c6a4fc763cd2820f2d
msgid "EKF_MAG_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:349
#: 6c0f008609334dc0805713afa5d31a64
msgid "This parameter scales the threshold used for the magnetometer measurement innovation consistency check. Decreasing it makes it more likely that good measurements will be rejected. Increasing it makes it more likely that bad measurements will be accepted. It is scaled in units of standard deviation. For example a setting of 3 means that differences greater than than 3 x the assumed standard deviation will cause the measurement to be rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:358
#: c62f14fe570c4cdba4291ebac8531c29
msgid "EKF_MAG_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:360
#: 5fb0fc37f51e49f49f898d1c4ebad57d
msgid "This is the RMS value of noise in magnetometer measurements / 1000. The magnetometer readings are scaled by 1/1000 before they are used by the filter to reduce the effect of numerical rounding errors. Increasing this noise parameter reduces the weighting on magnetometer measurements. This would make the filter yaw less affected less by magnetometer errors, but more affected by Z gyro drift. See the section on interpreting EKF3 log data for more information on using log data to help set this parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:370
#: 4c31537962484302a129f1bd9acaf997
msgid "EKF_MAX_FLOW"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:372
#: 55986864eed24cf5a683a983637809e8
msgid "This parameter controls the maximum amount of optical flow rate (in rad/sec) that will be accepted as a valid measurement by the EKF. This helps to reject measurements corrupted during data transfer or when the flow sensor is unable to keep up with the motion of the vehicle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:378
#: 7df6487e0eb3479d867f04b2649648de
msgid "EKF_POS_DELAY"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:380
#: a190243f3fc944fb806589bb3dea3f12
msgid "This is the number of msec that the GPS position measurements lag behind the inertial measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:384
#: e31eb8de33f0436c8dbe68881dacfa6a
msgid "EKF_POSNE_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:386
#: f0399700630f468fa889dd87b1e0db7f
msgid "This is the RMS value of noise in the GPS horizontal position measurements. If you increase this parameter, the filter will think the GPS is more noisy and will place less weighting on the horizontal GPS velocity measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:391
#: 81f5d723cfbe47e7b6bbb657dbb04e90
msgid "If this parameter is set to small, then the filter will constantly react to noise in the GPS position which can cause continual and rapid small attitude and position changes in copters during loiter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:395
#: b74295fcda094a3992937a835cfe9a5f
msgid "If this parameter is set to large, then the inertial sensor errors will cause the filter position to wander slowly as errors in the inertial calculations are not corrected enough by the GPS. This can cause excessive wander in position for copters during loiter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:404
#: 056ec6bd8591490aa505ddf72817667b
msgid "EKF_POS_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:406
#: fc695dc8bb3f4968a009fbbad3dcf348
msgid "This parameter scales the threshold used for the GPS position measurement innovation consistency check. Decreasing it makes it more likely that good measurements will be rejected. Increasing it makes it more likely that bad measurements will be accepted. It is scaled in units of standard deviation. For example a setting of 3 means that differences greater than than 3 x the assumed standard deviation will cause the measurement to be rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:415
#: f6976eb0839242b3b3e0a8d0992ee403
msgid "EKF_RNG_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:417
#: 5d3e13549e7040b5af55420a1543c9c8
msgid "This parameter controls the maximum amount of difference in between the measured range to ground and the predicted range before the EKF starts to reject the measurements. Reducing this parameter makes it more likely that valid range finder measurements will be rejected. Increasing this parameter makes it more likely that invalid range finder measurements will be accepted. It is scaled in units of standard deviation. For example a setting of 3 means that differences greater than than 3 x the assumed standard deviation will cause the measurement to be rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:427
#: 8093aeb9db5b42c8b7c2c0393e5f51b4
msgid "EKF_VELD_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:429
#: 924a4eb5718a42948c84b93cf2622b95
msgid "This is the RMS value of noise in the vertical GPS velocity measurement in m/s. If you increase this parameter, the filter will think the GPS is more noisy and will place less weighting on the vertical GPS velocity measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:434
#: 55a334b489974e5b940e2a6abce995c0
msgid "If this parameter is set too small, then the filter will constantly react to noise in the GPS measurement which will cause the filter height to be noisy. In copters this will cause the copter to jiggle up and down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:438
#: b4d22b12fa6646149c3367524a34838e
msgid "If this parameter is set too high then the filter will be not take full advantage of the GPS velocity information, and will be more susceptible to Barometer height glitches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:446
#: 6c07a5e1b89a4d3abad55a13dcd56198
msgid "EKF_VELNE_NOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:448
#: fe71156789064bfeb89af9dc076be3cc
msgid "This is the RMS value of noise in the North and East GPS velocity measurements in m/s. If you increase this parameter, the filter will think the GPS is more noisy and will place less weighting on the horizontal GPS velocity measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:453
#: 986346cc06634750a8f3ee94680954b3
msgid "If this parameter is set too small, then the filter will constantly react to noise in the GPS measurement which will cause the filter roll and pitch angles to be noisy. If you have the vehicle outside with a clear view of the sky and away from buildings and other large objects, then the HUD in mission planer should be steady. If it is moving around noticeably, then it is likely the GPS noise is too high for the filter setting. This will also result in continual and rapid small angle and position changes in copters during loiter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:462
#: 324c3c2c620f41178122125318a60052
msgid "If this parameter is set too high then the filter will be not take full advantage of the GPS velocity information, will wander more in position and will be more susceptible to GPS position glitches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:470
#: 4a1c75c9751640c4bb5965ef876b847f
msgid "EKF_VEL_DELAY"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:472
#: 0e01760fbeab4ede9c51147897c3b422
msgid "This is the number of msec that the GPS velocity measurements lag behind the inertial measurements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:476
#: 5d7ecf8389bd4bd7ab168b5e314c7a68
msgid "EKF_VEL_GATE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:478
#: 84b3101f120846e09c7c2751d3cad905
msgid "This parameter scales the threshold used for the GPS velocity measurement innovation consistency check. Decreasing it makes it more likely that good measurements will be rejected. Increasing it makes it more likely that bad measurements will be accepted. It is scaled in units of standard deviation. For example a setting of 3 means that differences greater than than 3 x the assumed standard deviation will cause the measurement to be rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:487
#: 9dfce2ace3644048b144a2faaf7a5c74
msgid "EKF_WIND_PNOISE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:489
#: d86d0deeddd24800b02cd93345bb28a4
msgid "This noise controls the growth of wind state error estimates. Increasing it makes wind estimation faster and noisier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:493
#: ff548f6b0aad4a2eb0403e3cc2878366
msgid "EKF_WIND_PSCALE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:495
#: b36b3771fedd481fabc815ff6d21f929
msgid "Increasing this parameter increases how rapidly the wind states adapt when changing altitude, but does make wind speed estimation noisier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:501
#: 43e0fb12f1304ec484ffe89f89ad939f
msgid "Interpreting Log Data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:503
#: e6cb9bae628e4f659453499f57277e0e
msgid "Correct tuning the Navigation filter is not possible without some analysis of the data logged by the filter in the flash logs. To log this data, it is important that AHRS data logging is enabled. The EKF data is contained in the EKF1, EKF2, EKF3 and EKF4 log messages. This section describes the meaning of the various EKF log data and shows examples obtained from plotting data using the Mission Planner DataFlash log review feature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:512
#: af953cf6c0fd4e1c81c7b567d79d83a6
msgid "EKF1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:514
#: 13e6869410e74e5094e7bce41a346a63
msgid "**TimeMS** - time in msec from startup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:516
#: 0d7d8d187e584adeafb41be6954c8917
msgid "**Roll** - Roll angle (deg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:518
#: 6092dbfe35cf475b8dff03c8a3f0a321
msgid "**Pitch** - Pitch angle (deg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:520
#: 439d69eb095a4ffd812a364fc8dc50be
msgid "**Yaw** - Yaw angle (deg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:522
#: 79c7861a94ed454cae3bdf87479cac15
msgid "**VN,VE,VD** - North,East,Down velocities (m/s)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:524
#: b47f593257d14e6287982c4906cf9023
msgid "**PN,PE,PD** - North,East,Down positions (m) relative to where the vehicle was armed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:527
#: 945f228ea6084d05b8158e3641c9beee
msgid "**GX,GY,GZ** - X,Y,Z Gyro biases (deg/min)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:529
#: 1a92a098199d45468db4847b17698e1e
msgid "The following figure shows the gyro biases from a plane with a Pixhawk controller. The gyro biases can be seen to vary at the start and stabilise about new values as the sensor warms up and reaches its operating temperature. The cheap MEMS inertial sensors used by our controllers can have significant bias variation with temperature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:539
#: 019e5364591c465aa21b823df2975f7e
msgid "EKF2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:541
#: 4ac9b5b949964bf5847a002b1f73965b
msgid "**TimeMS** - time in msec from startup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:543
#: ce82ee5b447b4cccaebe1bfa44951466
msgid "**Ratio** - Weighting percentage of the IMU1 accelerometer data used in the blending of IMU1 and IMU2 data. If two IMU's are available with your hardware (eg Pixhawk), then this will normally fluctuate rapidly in the 50% region as seen here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:551
#: c331e3498a6148c991119356e2645f1b
msgid "If it swings close to 100 or 0 % for parts of the flight, then this indicates that you likely have aliasing affecting your accelerometer data and you should look for solutions to reduce this (eg vibration isolation mounts for your autopilot)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:556
#: 60ce563bf36a49a48b592575407ab521
msgid "**AZ1bias** - Z accelerometer bias for IMU1 (cm/s:sup:`2`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:558
#: 826fac4868074727aeedd629ae755812
msgid "**AZ2bias** - Z accelerometer bias for IMU2 (cm/s:sup:`2`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:560
#: dce3bd52a83c4404abeac83c110873e6
msgid "**VWN,VWE** - North and East wind velocity (m/s). A positive value means the wind is moving in the direction of that axis, eg a positive North wind velocity is blowing from the South."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:564
#: 99831dddc71c4d7ea75a2074104cce8e
msgid "**MN,ME,MD** - North, East, Down earth magnetic field strength (sensor units). If you are flying quickly, or are at low speed with ``EKF_MAG_CAL`` enabled, these will slowly change during flight as the filter 'learns' the earth's magnetic field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:572
#: 3dc9f94adcdf4dbab0f99d8c0a609930
msgid "**MX,MY,MZ** - X, Y, Z body magnetic field biases (sensor units). If you are flying quickly, or are at low speed with ``EKF_MAG_CAL`` enabled, these will slowly change during flight as the filter 'learns' the earth's magnetic field. These have the same meaning as the compass offsets, but are the opposite sign (eg in the following figure MX stabilises at a value of +35, indicating that a ``COMPASS_OFS_X`` value of -35 should be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:584
#: a4065d5bcb1547bcb51ecbcfcd9e93b1
msgid "EKF3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:586
#: 55a05df2ef71433fbe5823610be05c47
msgid "This message contains the innovations for each sensor (GPS, barometer, magnetometer and airspeed). Innovations are the difference between the value predicted using the IMU data before corrections are applied, and the value measured by the sensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:591
#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:703
#: 4e148b57f3bc44faa6cd9e644752146c
#: f1fd6437bf604deb91d11e8a3be2d5fe
msgid "**TimeMS** - Time in msec from startup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:593
#: 24650928c36240c28a3c0fdeaaab4ef4
msgid "**IVN,IVE,IVD** - Innovations for the North,East,Down GPS velocity measurements (m/s). These are an important measure of health for the navigation filter. If you have god quality IMU and GPS data they will be small and around zero as shown in the following figure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:601
#: f4545e87f2b94b29b99e8716096d6796
msgid "The noise level on these innovations when the vehicle is not maneuvering can be used to set the value of ``EKF_VELNE_NOISE`` and ``EKF_VELD_NOISE``. For example in the above figure, the velocity noise when the vehicle was non-manoeuvring was around +-0.3 m/s for both the North,East and Down velocities. This means that a good starting value for \\ ``EKF_VELNE_NOISE`` and ``EKF_VELD_NOISE`` for this example would be 0.3 m/s."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:609
#: ddf525926b8c42d69211de09eaed591c
msgid "**IPN,IPE** - Innovations in the North, East GPS position measurements (m). Similarly to the velocity innovations, they should be small and centred on zero as in the following example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:616
#: 1013fbb6e69f422a8f78056aeb39750e
msgid "The noise levels on these innovations can be used to set the value of ``EKF_POSNE_NOISE``. In the above figure, the noise sits within a band of +-0.5m, so a good starting value for the value of ``EKF_POSNE_NOISE`` in this example would be 0.5m."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:621
#: fc607a646b564c2c8f2e466034e8d228
msgid "**IPD** - Innovations on the barometer height measurement (m). They should be small and centered on zero as in the following example, although transients of around 2m are common when sudden height changes or manoeuvres are performed due to IMU errors, sensor lag and the effect of changes in airflow on he barometer reading."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:630
#: ea039ba7525f4af0a06bcaf8ec71dac2
msgid "In the above figure it can be seen that there is a small 1m negative offset that is removed after 2min. This is due to bias errors on the Z accelerometers which take time to be learned by the filter and compensated for. In this example, the underlying sensor noise is low at about +-0.15m, which indicates a good starting value for ``EKF_ALT_NOISE`` for plane applications would be 0.15m."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:637
#: 4f223d8ff1b542cab77774d01fd5abd1
msgid "Note: For copter, experience has shown the value of ``EKF_ALT_NOISE`` normally has to be increased above the theoretical value to smooth out the height response"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:641
#: cb7ed487586644fe8acf3a5f9d5038a2
msgid "**IMX,IMY,IMZ** - Innovations for the Magnetometer X,Y,Z measurements. These should be centered around zero and not exceed +- 50 during manoeuvres as shown in the following figure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:648
#: a24415fa9a204c959515a76fc7acda54
msgid "In the above example ``EKF_MAG_CAL`` was set to 1, so the copter quickly learnt the magnetometer biases (compass offsets). Although the underlying noise of the magnetometer is relatively low (5 or less in most cases), there are other errors due to differences in scale factors between axes, magnetometer misalignment, and varying magnetic fields produced by electrical power systems that cause larger errors. Typically these result in sharp transients of about 50 in the innovations, as can be seen in the above figure. For this reason the default value of ``EKF_MAG_NOISE`` is set to 0.05 (which represents a noise of 50 in sensor units)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:659
#: 9797a6a0e2a34f9dbe4d57d9bb00088b
msgid "The following figure is taken from a slow speed copter flight with a bad magnetometer calibration and ``EKF_MAG_CAL`` = 0. The innovations vary noticeably as the vehicle changes its orientation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:666
#: 99d799d37c044186b2f7d1898235ddec
msgid "**IVT** - Innovation for the true airspeed measurement (m/s). This will be zero if the airspeed sensor is not fitted or is not being used (e.g. on ground). It should be centered around zero if the airspeed sensor is calibrated correctly, but will vary in noise level depending on how gusty the flight conditions are. the following is an example from a flight with a well calibrated airspeed sensor in moderate wind conditions of around 7m/s in low turbulence:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:677
#: f458f2ecc5984cda8e64dfc0b31bffbe
msgid "A constant offset of 1m/s from zero would indicate a steady 1m/s airspeed error. Steady airspeed errors can be caused if the airspeed sensor is uncovered during initialisation on a windy day resulting in a significant pressure offset, is out of cal, or has experienced a large change in temperature since initialisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:683
#: 57861aee19234b2d84f4353fe2f74f24
msgid "This figure can also be used to set the value for ``EKF_EAS_NOISE``. For the example shown above, the total noise (including gusts) is around 1.4 m/s, so this would be a good starting value for ``EKF_EAS_NOISE``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:688
#: fc6760e078cd447e9f683a84b37fae1a
msgid "EKF4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:690
#: 60d16b7441af4aa0bcc5645cc1bc1011
msgid "This message contains plots showing how each sensor is performing relative to the error gates set by the ``EKF_POS_GATE``, ``EKF_VEL_GATE``, ``EKF_HGT_GATE``, ``EKF_MAG_GATE`` and ``EKF_EAS_GATE``. These parameters control how inconsistent a measurement is allowed to be before the filter won't use it. When we refer to inconsistency of measurements in this section, we are talking about the amount of difference between the measurement predicted by the filter and the measurement taken by the sensor. Checking measurements for inconsistencies is particularly important with GPS, because GPS measurements can have very large transient position and velocity errors that would cause a crash if they were to be used by the filter. The following messages are available in EKF4:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:705
#: 61799be05b1543d1aea51553cc2115b3
msgid "**SV** - ratio of the combined GPS velocity inconsistency to the limit set by the ``EKF_VEL_GATE`` parameter. For a flight with good GPS data, this can have the occasional spike to over 1/2, but should never go above 1. If this line goes above 1, then it indicates that the filter stopped using the GPS velocity data for that period in flight. This should never happen with good sensor data. The following figure shows **SV** taken from a quadrotor flight with 9 to 10 satellites in good GPS conditions, using the default parameters. If this line is too high and goes above 1 with good GPS, then the ``EKF_VEL_GATE`` parameter should be increased."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:719
#: 3c745f169bbd446fabd93e0a7e2b34bf
msgid "**SP** - ratio of the GPS total position inconsistency to the limit set by the ``EKF_POS_GATE`` parameter. For a flight with good GPS data, this can have the occasional spike to over 1/2, but should never go above 1. If this line goes above 1, then it indicates that the filter stopped using the GPS position data for that period in flight. This should never happen with good sensor data. The following figure shows **SP** taken from a quadrotor flight with 9 to 10 satellites in good GPS conditions, using the default parameters. If this line is too high and goes above 1 with good GPS, then the ``EKF_POS_GATE`` parameter should be increased."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:733
#: 523f5162d85c45f9bd6aa338943650e9
msgid "**SH** - ratio of the barometer height inconsistency to the limit set by the ``EKF_HGT_GATE`` parameter. This can have the occasional spike to over 1/2, but should never go above 1. If this line goes above 1, then it indicates that the filter stopped using the barometer data for that period in flight. This should never happen with good sensor data. The following figure shows **SH** taken from a quadrotor flight at airspeeds up to 16 m/s, using the default parameters. If this line is too high and goes above 1, then the ``EKF_HGT_GATE`` parameter should be increased. Factors that can cause this to be high include airflow past the autopilot affecting the barometer reading and accelerometer errors due to sensor drift or aliasing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:748
#: 55e1f33255744cce8ecc00bc0b044b17
msgid "**SMX,SMY,SMZ** - ratio of the magnetometer X,Y and Z measurement inconsistencies to the limit set by the ``EKF_MAG_GATE`` parameter. This can have the occasional spike to over 1/2, but should never go above 1. If this line goes above 1, then it indicates that the filter stopped using that component of magnetometer data for that period in flight. This should never happen with good sensor data. The following figure shows the SMX, SMY and SMZ data taken from a quadrotor flight using the default parameters. If this line is too high and goes above 1 on a regular basis, then it indicates a problem with the compass calibration or installation. It is recommended that the reasons for the compass errors be investigated first before resorting to increasing the ``EKF_MAG_GATE`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:764
#: f6e442a0e4584a85ad2bd9f218a7661c
msgid "The next figure shows **SMX**, **SMY** and **SMZ** taken from a rover log, with the throttle demand **ThrOutR** also plotted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:770
#: e7e406bf2c83470386617a0f004be155
msgid "The large spikes above 1 every time the throttle steps up, and the large values during throttle operation can be clearly seen. In this example it would be recommended that steps be taken to reduce the amount of compass interference"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/extended-kalman-filter.rst:775
#: 202f760c493f40a093512936233cdd30
msgid "**SVT** - ratio of the airspeed measurement inconsistency to the limit set by the ``EKF_EAS_GATE`` parameter. This can have the occasional spike to over 1/2, but should rarely go above 1. If this line goes above 1, then it indicates that the filter stopped using the airspeed data for that period in flight. Factors that can cause this to be high include airspeed calibration errors, the presence of strong gusts and turbulence, and rapid changes in wind speed. It is normal for this to be higher at the start of the flight before the filter has estimated the wind velocity."
msgstr ""
